.\" Manual page for isotope-viewer
.TH ISOTOPE-VIEWER 1 "2026-10-31" "nntp2sql (beta 0.50)" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
isotope-viewer \- browse nntp2sql articles and yEnc files, read text articles, and view images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B isotope-viewer
.RB [ \-\-db\-type
.IR "sqlite" | "mysql" | "postgres"
.RI [ CONNECTION ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B isotope-viewer
is a GTK4 program for working with the headers that
.BR nntp2sql (1)
has stored. It lists the articles and the yEnc files they belong to, lets you search and queue files,
downloads and verifies them from the news server, reads text articles, and shows images in a built-in
viewer.
.PP
It works with SQLite, MySQL/MariaDB and PostgreSQL. Each backend starts with a connection string that
works on a default local installation, so on a stock setup you can open the program and click Open.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-db\-type " \fITYPE\fR [\fICONNECTION\fR]"
Start on the given backend, optionally with a connection string. Without it the program starts on the
backend and connection it used last, or on SQLite.
.SH CONNECTION STRINGS
.TP
.B SQLite
A path to a database file, or a directory containing several. Default:
.BR nntp2sql.db .
.TP
.B MySQL / MariaDB
.IR host,database,user,password,port .
Default:
.BR 127.0.0.1,nntp2sql,root,,3306 .
A URL form
.RI ( mysql://user:pass@host:port/db )
and a
.IR "host=\|... dbname=\|..." " form are also accepted."
.TP
.B PostgreSQL
Same forms as MySQL. Default:
.BR 127.0.0.1,nntp2sql,postgres,,5432 .
.PP
When the default host or user does not work, the program also tries
.B localhost
or
.BR 127.0.0.1 ,
and your operating-system user name.
.SH DETECTING DATABASES
On start, when the backend changes, and with the
.B Detect databases
button, the program looks for every database that has the nntp2sql tables and lists them in the
Database dropdown (for example
.BR news0 ,
.BR news1 ).
The first one found opens automatically. A database qualifies when it has an
.B articles
table with
.IR artnum ", " group_name " and " subject
columns and a
.B groups
table with
.IR name " and " article_count .
.SH THE WINDOW
.TP
.B Files / Articles / Text articles
.B Files
lists candidate yEnc files (parts grouped by subject).
.B Articles
lists every article.
.B "Text articles"
lists only text posts, meaning articles with no yEnc marker in the subject, arranged as discussion
threads: each reply sits directly under the post it answers, indented one level deeper and marked with an
arrow. Threads follow the References header; a reply whose parent is not in the loaded group starts its own
thread. Selecting a post shows its body automatically (the NNTP server and login must be filled in). Posts
in binary groups with obfuscated subjects have no yEnc marker either, so they are listed here too.
.TP
.B Group
Opening a database loads only the list of groups, not their articles. The dropdown starts on
\(lqSelect a group to load\(rq; choosing a group loads just that group's articles, and
\(lqAll groups (loads everything)\(rq loads the whole database, which is slow and memory-hungry on large
ones. Only groups that have articles are listed.
.TP
.B Search
Filters by subject, filename, author, group, date, Message-ID, article number, status, or the word
.BR queued .
.TP
.B Hide text articles
In Articles mode, hides text posts and shows only yEnc/binary articles. Unavailable in Text articles mode.
Remembered between runs.
.TP
.B Hide incomplete files
Hides files that are missing part headers or are ambiguous, in both modes. Remembered between runs.
.TP
.B Queue selected, Check all shown, Clear queue
Choose files for download.
.TP
.B Download file / queue, Download to...
Fetch the chosen files into the Save to folder (or a folder you pick). Each file is assembled from its
parts and checked against the decoded sizes and CRC32 before it is published. Existing files are never
overwritten. A file is saved only when it is complete: if a part's header is missing from the database, or a
part is no longer on the server, the file is refused with a message and nothing is written; incomplete files
are never saved partly.
.TP
.B Read article
Opens the selected article in its own window (see ARTICLE WINDOW). The selection may be any article; in Files
mode the file's first part is read.
.TP
.B Auto-load text articles
When on, selecting a single text article in Articles mode loads its body automatically. Text articles mode
always does this. If the body cannot be read, the details pane shows the reason.
.TP
.B View image
Download the selected image file (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp, tiff, avif, heic) and show it in an article
window. A file already saved this session opens without downloading again.
.TP
.B Save viewed images
When on, viewed images are also copied into the Save to folder; a name clash gets a numeric suffix
instead of an error. When off, nothing is kept on disk.
.SH ARTICLE WINDOW
Read article opens a window with the article's Subject, From, Date, Newsgroups and Message-ID, its text, and
a
.B Reply
button. The whole article is fetched from the news server (so the NNTP server and login must be set).
.PP
If the article carries an image, it is shown in the same window, below the text. Images are found as a
base64 attachment in a MIME message (including inside multipart messages) or as an inline yEnc body; for
yEnc the checksum is verified and a warning is shown if it does not match. Text in other character sets and
quoted-printable text are converted. Attachments that are not shown, and any further images, are listed in
a note under the text.
.PP
The image area has
.BR "Zoom in" ,
.BR "Zoom out" ,
.B 100%
and
.B Fit
buttons and
.BR "Save image..." ,
which writes the original file bytes to a place you choose. Several windows can be open at once.
.SH REPLYING
.B Reply
opens an editor with the sender, Newsgroups (the group you are browsing), Subject (\(lqRe: ...\(rq without
repeating Re:) and the original text quoted with \(lq> \(rq, cut at the signature. The References header is
built from the original's, ending with its Message-ID, so threads stay connected. Enter your address in
.B From
(\(lqName <you@example.com>\(rq; it is remembered), write below the quote, and press
.BR Post .
.PP
Posting is public and cannot be taken back, so a confirmation always appears first, naming the group and the
server. The article is sent through the server entered in the main window with NNTP POST, as UTF-8; non-ASCII
names and subjects are encoded. The Post button is disabled after a successful post so the same reply cannot
be sent twice. Header fields cannot contain line breaks. The server assigns the Message-ID and Date.
.SH DEFAULTS FROM NNTP2SQL
Server details are filled in from, in this order of priority:
.IP 1. 3
The first time you open a database, the options stored in it by
.B nntp2sql \-\-save\-config\-db
(server, port, security, workers, user, password). They are kept in a table named after the database, such as
.B news0_settings
for the database
.BR news0 ;
databases from older versions keep them in a table called
.BR settings ,
which is read when the named table is empty.
.IP 2. 3
At launch, the file
.B ~/.config/nntp2sql/nntp2sql.conf
written by
.B nntp2sql \-\-save\-config
(including the database connection).
.IP 3. 3
The viewer's own remembered settings in
.BR ~/.config/nntp2sql/viewer.ini .
.PP
Opening the same database again does not overwrite fields you have edited.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/nntp2sql/nntp2sql.conf
Options saved by nntp2sql. Contains passwords.
.TP
.B ~/.config/nntp2sql/viewer.ini
Folder, server, security, workers, database connections and the checkbox settings last used.
.SH BUGS AND LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
The passwords read from nntp2sql's file and database are stored in plain text.
.IP \(bu 2
Image detection uses the file name in the subject. A file with an image extension that is not a real
image is saved if
.B Save viewed images
is on and then reported as unsupported.
.IP \(bu 2
Downloads refuse to overwrite an existing file, so fetching a file that already exists in the Save to
folder fails with an error.
.IP \(bu 2
Choosing \(lqAll groups\(rq reads every article into memory; very large databases take time and memory.
Loading one group at a time avoids this.
.IP \(bu 2
Threads are built only from the References header, within the loaded group, and the threaded view is
Text articles mode only. Long threads are indented to at most 24 levels.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the first image of an article is shown, and uuencoded attachments are not decoded.
.IP \(bu 2
Posting supports plain replies only: no new threads, cancels or attachments, and the Message-ID and Date are
left to the server. Whether posting is allowed depends on the server and account.
.IP \(bu 2
The viewer cannot confirm that a post appeared; it reports only the server's answer.
.IP \(bu 2
PostgreSQL support is loaded at run time from libpq and is the least exercised backend.
.SH ICON
The program has its own icon, a newspaper, named
.BR isotope-viewer .
On Linux and BSD, GTK finds it in the icon theme (installed under
.IR prefix /share/icons/hicolor ,
with the matching
.B .desktop
file in
.IR prefix /share/applications ),
or next to the program in an
.B icons
folder when run from a build directory. On macOS the Dock icon is set while the program runs. On Windows the
window icon comes from the same icon theme and the executable's own icon is compiled in from
.BR icons/isotope-viewer.rc .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nntp2sql (1),
.BR hydrogen\-group\-viewer (1)
